1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,562
LeetCode problem number one, and the twist that

2
00:00:03,562 --> 00:00:06,524
trips people up: it asks for indices, not values.

3
00:00:06,474 --> 00:00:09,575
Let's nail it the way interviewers expect.

4
00:00:19,844 --> 00:00:22,926
Given an integer array nums and a target, return the

5
00:00:22,926 --> 00:00:26,008
indices of the two numbers that add up to the target.

6
00:00:25,958 --> 00:00:31,030
There is exactly one valid answer, and you may not use the same element twice.

7
00:00:34,796 --> 00:00:40,203
For example, nums three, two, four with target six returns indices one and two.

8
00:00:40,153 --> 00:00:44,888
Note that three plus three is not allowed, because that would reuse index zero.

9
00:00:44,888 --> 00:00:48,867
Because we need indices, sorting the array is off the table.

10
00:00:52,628 --> 00:00:55,590
The brute force tries every pair i less than j,

11
00:00:55,590 --> 00:00:58,552
and checks whether nums i plus nums j equals the target.

12
00:00:58,502 --> 00:01:01,831
That's O of n squared time and O of one space.

13
00:01:05,588 --> 00:01:10,129
The insight: for each x, the partner we need is target minus x.

14
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:13,162
If we remember every value we've passed, mapped to

15
00:01:13,162 --> 00:01:16,245
its index, we can find the partner in constant time.

16
00:01:17,812 --> 00:01:20,935
So keep a HashMap from value to index.

17
00:01:20,885 --> 00:01:24,365
For each index i, if the map contains the complement, return

18
00:01:24,365 --> 00:01:27,844
the stored index and i. Otherwise store nums i and continue.

19
00:01:31,612 --> 00:01:34,927
Trace three, two, four with target six.

20
00:01:34,877 --> 00:01:39,300
Index zero: need three, map is empty, store three at zero.

21
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:43,183
Index one: need four, missing, store two at one.

22
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:46,704
Index two: need two, found at index one.

23
00:01:46,704 --> 00:01:48,940
Return one, two.

24
00:01:52,708 --> 00:01:56,684
In Java, the map is a HashMap of Integer to Integer.

25
00:01:56,634 --> 00:02:00,096
Inside the loop we look up target minus nums i.

26
00:02:00,096 --> 00:02:03,666
If it's not null, we return the pair of indices.

27
00:02:03,666 --> 00:02:07,428
Below it, I kept the brute force version for comparison.

28
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:16,463
One pass over the array gives O of n time, and the map uses O of n extra space.

29
00:02:20,164 --> 00:02:23,372
Two pitfalls: return indices, not values, and

30
00:02:23,372 --> 00:02:26,580
don't sort, because it destroys the original indices.

31
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:31,122
The solution and tests are on the leetcode branch of the AlgoExpert repo.

32
00:02:31,122 --> 00:02:34,199
I'm Waqar, see you in the next problem.

